<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gangadhar Rao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1853">1853</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gangadhar Rao was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> Raja of Jhansi of the Newalkar family, who ruled until his death in 1853. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmibai">Manikarnika</a>, later famous as Rani Lakshmibai. Shortly before his death he adopted a son, Damodar Rao, but the governor-general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> refused to recognise the adoption and annexed Jhansi under the doctrine of lapse in 1854.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao.jpg" alt="Gangadhar Rao" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gangadhar_Rao_alias_Babasaheb_Newalkar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gangadhar Rao Newalkar was born on 4 April 1797, a Karhade Brahmin whose ancestors came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a> district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>; some of them moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> rule began and held high posts in the Peshwa and Holkar armies. He was the son of Shiv Rao Bhau and a descendant of Raghunath Hari Newalkar, the first governor of Jhansi under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> rule. Jhansi, part of the wider region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>, had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Maratha Empire until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> became its overlord in 1804, during the Second Anglo Maratha War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death in 1838 of Raghunath Rao III, his brother and the son of Shiv Rao Bhau, the British accepted Gangadhar Rao as Raja of Jhansi in 1843. He restored the state's finances, which had declined under his predecessor, and took steps for the growth of the town of Jhansi, whose architecture he enriched. He commanded an army of about 5,000 men and gathered a library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> manuscripts, and his statecraft is said to have impressed the British.</p><p>His first wife, Ramabai, died early and never became queen consort, since he took the title of Raja only after her death. On 19 May 1842 he married the young Manikarnika Tambe, renamed Lakshmibai, who was given the title of Rani on her marriage. In December 1851 she bore a son, Damodar Rao, who died in March 1852.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On the day before he died, Gangadhar Rao adopted Anand Rao, the son of his cousin Vasudev Newalkar of Parola, and renamed him Damodar Rao. The adoption took place before the British political officer, who was handed a letter from the Raja asking that the child be treated with kindness and that Jhansi be governed by his widow for her lifetime. He died on 21 November 1853. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmibai">Lakshmibai</a> went on to lead Jhansi against the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a> and became a symbol of resistance to Company rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gangadhar Rao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raja <b>Gangadhar Rao</b> adopted a son shortly before his death in 1853, but Lord Dalhousie refused to recognise the adoption.</blockquote><a href="/state/jhansi-state">Jhansi State</a></li></ul></section>
